# a-defended-base — plan 12 B7, the phase's headline.
#
# `an-undefended-base.keys` plus five `click`s: a wall sealing the band
# at q = 5.  Same ground, same core, same spawn, same wave list, same
# everything — so the difference in the clock is the wall and can be
# nothing else.
#
#   undefended:   61 ticks   ( 41 s)
#   this file:   104 ticks   ( 69 s)  — 70% longer
#
# ⚠ **A SEAL, not a funnel — and the distinction is the finding.**  The
# same wall with a one-hex gate left open in it falls in **62 ticks**
# against the undefended base's 61, because walking through a gate costs
# an attacker essentially nothing.  A wall buys time only where it has to be
# CHEWED.  `plans/12` § B7 has the measurement; what a player learns
# from it is that an entrance is a decision about their own convenience,
# never a defence.
#
# ── The ending this one reaches: THE WALL BROKE, AT ITS WEAK END ─
#
# `plans/12` § What this plan does NOT build asks B7 to report which of
# two endings takes the base.  This is the first: the wave chews the
# wall at 1 HP/s each until a hex runs out, it comes down into a heap of
# masonry, and they walk through the hole.  Nobody dies, so no body ever
# piles — which is exactly why this base gets the long clock.
# `a-tower-buries-its-own-wall.keys` is the other ending.
#
# ⚠⚠ **It breaks at (5, -2), an END worth 30 HP — not at the braced
# middle — and that is plan 11 F7b arriving.**  `ENEMY_MOVEMENT.md` § A
# wall's HP is structural always claimed enemies *"spread along the
# perimeter and chew everywhere at once, so they do not need to FIND the
# weak hex"*; plan 12 B3 measured the opposite and recorded it as
# latent, because a queued wave met the wall at one point.  A blocked
# enemy now steps beside, so the wave arrives as a FRONT five hexes
# wide, lands equal damage across the face, and the weakest hex under it
# runs out first.  Nothing coordinates it and no enemy knows what
# bracing is.
#
# ⚠ **The front has a width, and a longer wall still hides its ends.**
# Measured: the same eight robots against a SEVEN-hex wall land nothing
# on either end, because the fan is not that wide.  So the bracing rule
# still rewards a perimeter longer than the wave can reach across.
#
# ⚠ **The unzip is visible here.**  (5, -1) starts braced at 100 HP and
# drops to 30: bracing is computed from the world rather than stored, so
# the hex beside a fresh breach becomes an END and loses 70% of its
# allowance the moment its neighbour goes (plan 12 B3).

echo a defended base: a sealed wall, and it nearly doubles the clock

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -3 -2 12 -2
drag -3 -1 12 -1
drag -3 0 12 0
drag -3 1 12 1
drag -3 2 12 2
count painted 80 80

# The seal: five hexes across the whole band, no gate.
# ⚠ A `wall` is 3.0 m and a robot climbs 2.0, so there is no way over
# it either — B0's four constraints put the climb below a bare wall on
# purpose.
palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 0
click 5 1
click 5 2
kind 5 0 wall
count painted 80 80

# ⚠ **100 HP, and it is the BRACED figure.**  (5, 0) has a structure
# neighbour on either side along the column, so it gets the full
# allowance; the two ends at (5, ±2) get 30.  `a-wall-breaks.keys`'s
# lone plug in a corridor got 15 — same `numbers.json` number, three
# different answers, because a wall's HP is structural (plan 12 B3).
hp 5 0 100.0 100.0
hp 5 2 30.0 30.0

# The same two waves as the control, off `numbers.json`'s own list and
# spaced by its own `inter_wave_delay`.  ⚠ A third wave of 12 was
# measured and changes the clock by NOTHING — see the control's note.
wave 5
count alive 5 5
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 8
count alive 13 13

fall 800
ticks 107 117
wallet 0.0 0.0

# ── The ending, stated ──────────────────────────────────────────

# The wall is GONE from (5, -2) — `break_structure` repaints it, because
# a broken wall really is gone rather than shadowed behind an overlay.
kind 5 -2 grass

# And it left a heap.  ⚠ This is the half that makes the breach a way
# IN: had the break ERASED the hex, the painted layer is sea-default,
# so the breach would have read as `sea` and been strictly LESS passable
# than the wall it replaced (plan 12 § Open question 5).
pile 5 -2 0.9 1.1

# ⚠ **The braced middle is still standing, at two thirds of its HP.**
# That is the bracing rule paying out: equal damage everywhere, and the
# 30 HP end ran out while the 100 HP middle did not.
kind 5 0 wall
hp 5 0 65.0 75.0

# The unzip: braced at 100 before the breach, an end at 30 after it.
hp 5 -1 30.0 30.0

# Still nobody has died.  That is what separates this base from the
# next one, and it is the whole reason this one holds twice as long.
count alive 13 13

snap b7_a_defended_base
